{
 "duration": 58.184,
 "sentences": [
  {
   "index": 0,
   "start": 0.25,
   "end": 3.12,
   "text": "A Docker container is not a tiny virtual machine."
  },
  {
   "index": 1,
   "start": 3.34,
   "end": 5.804,
   "text": "It's just a Linux process, wearing a disguise."
  },
  {
   "index": 2,
   "start": 6.224,
   "end": 11.587,
   "text": "A virtual machine boots a full guest operating system, with its own kernel. Heavy, and slow."
  },
  {
   "index": 3,
   "start": 11.967,
   "end": 17.518,
   "text": "A container skips all of that. It shares the host's kernel, and relies on three kernel features."
  },
  {
   "index": 4,
   "start": 17.898,
   "end": 25.968,
   "text": "One: namespaces. They control what the process can see. Inside, it thinks it's process ID one, with its own network and hostname."
  },
  {
   "index": 5,
   "start": 26.348,
   "end": 34.908,
   "text": "Two: cgroups. They limit what it can use. Half a CPU, five hundred megabytes of RAM. Exceed the memory, and the kernel kills it."
  },
  {
   "index": 6,
   "start": 35.288,
   "end": 42.332,
   "text": "Three: a layered filesystem. Your image is a stack of read-only layers, with one thin writable layer on top."
  },
  {
   "index": 7,
   "start": 42.712,
   "end": 48.212,
   "text": "Don't believe me? Run ps on the host. Your container is right there, an ordinary process."
  },
  {
   "index": 8,
   "start": 48.592,
   "end": 53.48,
   "text": "That's why containers start in a fraction of a second, and one server can run hundreds of them."
  },
  {
   "index": 9,
   "start": 53.93,
   "end": 57.284,
   "text": "A container isn't a machine. It's a process with boundaries."
  }
 ],
